# encoder_intra_smooth_paeth_2026-08-03 — provenance
# Companion to encoder_intra_smooth_paeth_2026-08-03.md and its data files.
#
# The named follow-up of KB-PERF-4 (benchmarks/encoder_intra_dir_i16_2026-08-03.md
# §1b / §8): "smooth + paeth, +2.63 ms, already i32x8 where libaom is u8/u16".
# Unlike the directional sub-lever, this one IS the bd8 lane-width programme —
# there is a real lane-width choice to make here and the port was on the wide
# side of it.
#
# Order of work, which is the point of the exercise (DIFFERENTIAL_PLAYBOOK §14):
#   1. re-profile the row at the CURRENT baseline;
#   2. census what a column kernel can reach, by MODE and by BLOCK WIDTH;
#   3. derive the lane bound and check it exhaustively, before writing a kernel;
#   4. write the kernel, gate it on the bound at runtime, prove the gate bites
#      AND fires, prove the integration differential reaches it;
#   5. measure, with nulls on both sides, EACH HALF SEPARATELY;
#   6. revert the half that measured nothing.

date                 2026-08-03
box                  Apple M4 Pro, 12 cores, 24 GiB, macOS 26.5.2 (Darwin arm64)
baseline commit      1f366a9 (main at session start) — every band's `base` arm
                     is built from this tree with the gates forced false
landing commit       see the git log entry that carries this file. It was
                     REBASED onto 5884f49 before pushing (main moved during the
                     session: a census extension, a screen-content content, and
                     KB-36's >=1080p default_min_partition_size fix). Verified
                     after the rebase: the profile cell still encodes to the
                     same 4472-byte stream by sha256, and KB-36's arm is
                     >=1080p so this 1024x1024 cell cannot reach it. The bands
                     were taken pre-rebase and are NOT re-taken; that is the one
                     place where the measured tree and the pushed tree differ,
                     and the byte check is the evidence they agree where it
                     matters.
worktree             zenav1-aom--smooth, detached (removed at the end of the session)
libaom oracle        upstream/ submodule 03087864cf4bea6abb0d28f95cf7843511413d8f (v3.14.1)
                     libaom.a borrowed READ-ONLY from the primary checkout's
                     upstream/build (this worktree's submodule is a fresh clone
                     with no build tree; nothing in the primary was written)
rustc                stable toolchain; no -C target-cpu=native (runtime SIMD dispatch)
threading            single-threaded on every arm
nice                 every measured run under `nice -n 19`, `-j 4`
box load             SHARED with two other agents for part of the session; every
                     `uptime` is recorded beside the band it belongs to in the .md.
                     The bands were taken in the quietest window available and
                     the raw spreads are reported rather than smoothed.

## Profile cell — unchanged from all five prior encoder studies, for continuity

1024x1024 photo, cq-level 44, cpu-used 6, ALLINTRA (usage 2), 8-bit 4:2:0,
single tile, single thread. Source $HOME/tmp/xb/src/photo_1024.yuv. Every arm
emits the same 4472-byte stream — verified by SHA, not by byte count.

## Part 1 — the re-profile of the row (no change in the tree)

Method identical to encoder_intra_dir_i16_2026-08-03.meta so the two compare:
/usr/bin/sample, 1 ms, 60 s window started 5 s in; self(node) = count -
sum(children); symbols classified by name. Port sampled TWICE (run 1 is the
stability control, run 2 is the one the table uses), libaom-c once.

  (cd benchmarks/xbench && nice -n 19 cargo build --release -j 4 -p drv-aom)
  nice -n 19 cc -O2 -o benchmarks/xbench/target/drv_libaom \
      benchmarks/xbench/csrc/drv_libaom.c \
      -I<primary>/upstream -I<primary>/upstream/build \
      -L<primary>/upstream/build -laom -lm -lc++
  (cd benchmarks/xbench && CARGO_TARGET_DIR=$HOME/tmp/smooth-dbg-target \
      CARGO_PROFILE_RELEASE_DEBUG=2 nice -n 19 cargo build --release -j 4 -p drv-aom)
  dsymutil $HOME/tmp/smooth-dbg-target/release/drv-aom

  nice -n 19 bash scripts/eprof_control.sh 9 ~/tmp/smooth/control_base.tsv
  PORT_DBG=$HOME/tmp/smooth-dbg-target/release/drv-aom \
    nice -n 19 bash scripts/eprof_sample.sh port-dbg 600 60 ~/tmp/smooth/port1.sample.txt 5
  nice -n 19 bash scripts/eprof_sample.sh libaom-c 1600 60 ~/tmp/smooth/libaomc.sample.txt 5
  PORT_DBG=... nice -n 19 bash scripts/eprof_sample.sh port-dbg 600 60 ~/tmp/smooth/port2.sample.txt 5
  python3 scripts/eprof_rollup.py ~/tmp/smooth/port2.sample.txt   --arm port     --rules rust --tsv ~/tmp/smooth/port2.tsv
  python3 scripts/eprof_rollup.py ~/tmp/smooth/libaomc.sample.txt --arm libaom-c --rules c    --tsv ~/tmp/smooth/c.tsv

Denominators are the medians of the timed reps taken DURING the sample runs
(port 155.101 ms of 600, libaom-c 47.202 ms of 1600). `sample` suspends the
target and inflates the port arm, which is why the clean interleaved control
band (§3) is reported separately and is the number the ratio is quoted at.

The like-for-like class table (.stage.tsv) is a symbol regex over the two
rollups, NOT the stage rollup — the stage rules file the port's cfl under
`dsp:intra-pred` and libaom's under `intra-mode-rd`. Regexes are in the .md.

## Part 2 — the census

NOT throwaway this time. `aom_dsp::census` (feature `census`, default off)
gained `Counts::nd_mode_tx_calls` / `nd_mode_tx_px` — the non-directional mode
axis split by transform shape — and `examples/content_census.rs` gained the
`nd_mode_x_tx` and `nd_mode_x_width` tables. A column-vectorized predictor is
priced by BLOCK WIDTH (how much of a lane vector it fills), and the previous
census could not report that. Both are committed.

  cargo build --release -j 4 -p zenav1-aom-bench --features census,c-oracle \
      --example content_census
  ./target/release/examples/content_census \
      yuv:$HOME/tmp/xb/src/photo_1024.yuv:1024x1024 \
      winperf:photo winperf:detail winperf:smooth      > .census.txt

## Part 3 — the audit

  python3 xtask/audit_nd16_lanes.py        (committed; output in .audit.txt)

Exhaustive enumeration, not an inequality: every intermediate is a function of
at most four small scalars. It reports M* per kernel, the binding intermediate,
the witness at M*+1 that shows the bound is tight, and a COMPLETE sweep of the
halving-add identity over every reachable A+B (0..130560). No bound was widened.

## Part 4 — the arms and the FIVE bands

Arms are separate release binaries built from this worktree by forcing some of
`intra::simd16`'s gate predicates to `false` and restoring from a pristine copy
(`scripts/smooth_build_arms.sh`, committed; SHA-verified distinct). While PAETH was still in
the tree the set was seven:

  base        all gates false — the pre-lever baseline INCLUDING the absence of
              the gate's per-block scan
  baseB       a SECOND COPY of the identical `base` binary — the box's noise
              floor on the PRE side, measured rather than asserted (§6)
  smoothonly  the SMOOTH-family gates live, PAETH's forced false.  THIS IS THE
              FUNCTIONAL EQUIVALENT OF WHAT SHIPPED (PAETH's kernel is
              dead-code-eliminated behind a literal `false`), and it is the arm
              the headline number comes from.
  paethonly   PAETH's gate live, the three SMOOTH gates forced false
  all         SMOOTH + PAETH composed
  allB        a SECOND COPY of `all` — the noise floor on the POST side
  libaom-c    benchmarks/xbench/csrc/drv_libaom.c against the pinned oracle

  nice -n 19 bash scripts/eprof_ab.sh <N> <out.tsv> base=... baseB=... \
      smoothonly=... paethonly=... all=... allB=... libaom-c=...
  ROTATE=1 nice -n 19 bash scripts/eprof_ab.sh <N> <out.tsv> ...     # band 4, F
  python3 scripts/eprof_ab_stats.py <tsv> --ref libaom-c --vs base

Five bands, none averaged into another:

  .ab.tsv               band 1, 7 arms x 44 rounds, fixed order, CONTENDED
                        (raw spreads 10.6-13.6 %)
  .ab_paethstore.tsv    band 2, 7 arms x 36 rounds, fixed order — the PAETH
                        store-shape A/B (`paethA` per-lane loop vs `paethB`
                        bitcast_u16x16 + copy_from_slice)
  .ab_fixedorder.tsv    band 3, 7 arms x 44 rounds, fixed order, QUIET box
                        (raw spreads 1.6-2.2 %) — THE HEADLINE BAND
  .ab_rotated.tsv       band 4, 7 arms x 49 rounds, ROTATE=1, contended — the
                        position-balanced band that convicts PAETH
  (not committed)       band F, 5 arms x 45 rounds, ROTATE=1, on the literal
                        shipped binary: landed in a 65-80 % spread window and is
                        NOT quoted.  Raw TSV kept at ~/tmp/smooth/abF_contended.tsv.

The two halves are measured separately even though they were built together
(§14's closing rule) — and that is what caused PAETH to be reverted.

## Part 4b — the harness change this session forced

`scripts/eprof_ab.sh` gained **`ROTATE=1`** (default off) plus a `position`
column in its TSV, and `scripts/eprof_ab_stats.py` was made tolerant of both TSV
shapes so previously recorded bands still parse.

Reason, measured: in band 3 the two copies of ONE identical binary at round
positions 5 and 6 came out **0.34 pp apart** while the copies at positions 1 and
2 agreed to 0.11 pp. In a fixed-order interleave the position an arm occupies is
worth as much as the effect being measured. With rotation the drift becomes a
property of the round rather than of any arm and is directly measurable: pooled
over all arms in band 4, position 1 runs 162.45 ms and position 6 runs
159.67 ms, a 1.7 % gradient.

## Byte-identity gate

  AOM_CONFORMANCE_DIR=<primary checkout>/conformance/data \
    cargo nextest run --cargo-profile test-fast -j 4 --workspace \
      --no-fail-fast --run-ignored all                     # SIMD dispatch live
  AOM_FORCE_SCALAR=1 <same>                                # scalar-pinned
  cargo check --target x86_64-apple-darwin --workspace --all-targets

This worktree borrows the primary checkout's gitignored conformance/data
READ-ONLY via AOM_CONFORMANCE_DIR; without it ~40 tests fail-loud with
"conformance vector missing", which is the fail-loud design working.

RESULTS: 972/972 passed, 0 skipped, in BOTH dispatch modes on the tree the bands
were taken on (769 s SIMD-live, 893 s scalar-pinned), and 986/986 in BOTH modes
after the rebase onto 5884f49 (928 s / 1129 s).  The count grew because other
landings added tests, not because anything here did.

Gate 2 (`config_permutations`, every --cpu-used 0..9 cell byte-exact against
real aomenc) is inside `--run-ignored all` and was also run on its own.

## Bite proofs — and they are what proves the differentials REACH the new code

Two perturbations, each applied alone, `--no-fail-fast` so every binary runs:

  A. SMOOTH's truncating halving add -> the ROUNDING one
     `(a & b) + ((a ^ b) >> 1)`  ->  `(a | b) - ((a ^ b) >> 1)`
     (i.e. floor((A+B)/2) -> ceil((A+B)/2), the exact thing the audit's sweep
     distinguishes, first wrong at A+B = 255)
     FAILS: simd16::every_kernel_matches_the_scalar_core, build_nd_diff,
            intra_lowbd_diff, intra_simd_diff, predict_intra_diff
     GREEN: highbd_diff (bd10/12 ONLY — the SMOOTH gate declines there, so it
            MUST stay green), intra_diff (the u8 `predict` path, untouched),
            simd16::paeth_*, dir_simd_diff

  B. PAETH's tie order  `p_top.simd_le(p_tl)` -> `.simd_lt(p_tl)`
     FAILS: simd16::paeth_matches_the_scalar_core_across_every_bit_depth,
            build_nd_diff, highbd_diff (PAETH's bound admits bd10/12, so it is
            reached there too), intra_lowbd_diff, intra_simd_diff,
            predict_intra_diff
     GREEN: simd16::every_kernel_matches_the_scalar_core (the SMOOTH half),
            intra_diff, dir_simd_diff + dir_highbd_diff (the directional family
            must not see a non-directional defect)

  B was taken while the PAETH kernel was still in the tree.  It is kept in the
  record because it is the evidence that highbd_diff flips with the BOUND
  (PAETH's admitted bd10/12, SMOOTH's does not) rather than with the kernel.

The asymmetry is the point: A and B hit disjoint sets of the in-crate
differentials, the bd10/12 integration test flips with the bound rather than
with the kernel, and the u8 and directional suites stay green through both.

## x86-64 / Windows

  gh workflow run winperf.yml -f base_sha=5884f4942b5bb6ca0706276b3bcb15fce3ef550d \
      -f rounds=24 -f arms=prepost -f contents="detail photo"

`base_sha` is the commit IMMEDIATELY BEFORE this landing, not the session's
profiling baseline (1f366a9): main moved during the session, and `post - pre`
has to isolate THIS lever rather than everything that landed alongside it.

Run 30819647374. RESULT: RESOLVED on `windows-11-arm` on both contents
(-0.961 % `detail` at 2.66x its floor, -0.512 % `photo` at 1.70x, 22/24 rounds
each, p < 0.0001); NOT resolvable on `windows-latest` x86-64 (`detail` -0.058 %
at 0.14x floor; `photo` +0.362 % at 0.85x floor -- wrong sign, under the floor,
named as the open item in the .md sec 8). Allocator censuses identical to the
digit on every arm on both runners.

Content chosen from the census, not by habit: smooth+paeth is 50.6 % of
predicted pixels on `detail`, 39.1 % on `photo`, 38.3 % on `smooth`, against the
study photograph's 43.6 %. Every winperf content reaches this lever — the
opposite of KB-PERF-4, where `detail` was a structural zero. Bands committed as
.win_<runner>_<content>.tsv; read with scripts/winperf_prepost_stats.py, which
handles the within-round position drift.

Known runner floors (benchmarks/winperf_content_census_2026-08-03.md §5): the
smallest effect resolvable at 95 %, n=24, is 0.07 % on Darwin, 0.07-0.18 % on
windows-11-arm and 0.50-0.86 % on windows-latest x86-64.

## Known gaps

* ONE cell, one image, one content class, one preset. cpu-used 0/3/4/9 are not
  measured.
* 8-bit 4:2:0 only. The SMOOTH gate declines bd10/bd12 by construction, so the
  narrow SMOOTH path is UNMEASURED at those depths (it never runs); PAETH's
  admits them but no bd10/12 timing was taken.
* SMOOTH_V and SMOOTH_H are shipped with a correctness gate and NO timing
  evidence: the census says the encoder picks them ZERO times at this cell on
  all four sources. They are in the landing because they are the same three
  lines and the same bound, not because anything measured them.
* The cost of the gate's DECLINE (one O(bw+bh) scan that then takes the i32x8
  path) is folded into the reported win, not isolated — no arm was built with
  the scan present and the vector path disabled. KB-PERF-4 measured that cost
  for its own gate at about +0.15 % on content its kernel never reaches.
* Wall clock only. No instruction count (no valgrind on Apple Silicon).
* Single-threaded, one frame.
* The lowbd `u8` predictors (`intra::predict`, the decoder's bd8 path) are
  untouched — this is an encoder lever and the encoder holds planes as u16.
* Linux is unmeasured.

